        "articleSummary": "Scientists are developing a vaccine produced via mosquitoes to immunize bats against Nipah and Rabies. The approach uses mosquitoes carrying vaccine-like elements in their saliva, which can reach bats when they bite or are ingested. In laboratory studies with mice and bats, this delivery method elicited antibodies against Nipah and Rabies, indicating potential protective immunity. The aim is to reduce bat-to-human spillover of infections by vaccinating wildlife through their insect vectors. If proven safe and effective, this strategy could offer a novel way to curb animal-to-human transmission. Current results are preliminary and limited to controlled lab settings, requiring further safety and efficacy testing before any field use.",
