        "articleSummary": "Leishmaniasis control intensifies in a Brazilian municipality. In 2025, 562 animal samples were tested; 81 rapid tests were positive and 36 were laboratory-confirmed, mainly in dogs, which show hair loss, overgrown nails, skin lesions, and weight loss. Suspected canine infections trigger a ~20-minute rapid test; confirmed cases undergo blood collection for Lacen confirmation, followed by euthanasia to prevent parasite persistence. Surveillance covers vector-active areas, using overnight sand fly traps to map transmission risk. Chemical control uses residual insecticides in cycles (Jan–May and Aug–Dec), potentially extending up to two years in some locales. A concurrent canine survey (inquérito canino) operates. Forty-seven areas are monitored, with higher activity in Matinha, Genipapo, Ipuaçu, Fazenda Umbuzeiro and several urban neighborhoods (Campo do Gado Novo, Aviário, George Américo, Queimadinha, Caseb, Feira VII, Gabriela, Rua Nova, Caraíbas, Pampalona, Novo Horizonte). No human cases were reported in 2025; surveillance continues in 2026; transmission is vector-borne by sand flies, not person-to-person; forms include visceral and cutaneous.",
