        "articleSummary": "133.7 tons of potential dengue breeding sites eliminated in the Ucayali region, Peru, this year under the Ministry of Health’s Dengue Prevention and Control Plan 2026, across eight districts: Raimondi (224.1), Calleria (33.5), Manantay (21.4), Monte Alegre (23.4), Yarinacocha (21.2), Campo Verde (2.7), Padre Abad (6.9), and Alexander Von Humbolt (0.6) tons. Campaigns targeted containers that collect rainwater—coconut shells, bottles, tires, old pots, toys, and other discarded receptacles. Health networks Aguaytía-San Alejandro, Atalaya, Coronel Portillo, and Federico Basadre trained residents to identify and eliminate breeding sites, including washing, scrubbing, and covering water containers. Dengue, transmitted by Aedes aegypti, presents with high fever, headache, muscle/joint pain, retro-orbital pain, and skin rashes; alarm signs include severe abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, bleeding, drowsiness, irritability, and weakness. Seek care promptly and avoid self-medication.",
