        "articleSummary": "Beijing-based scientists are pursuing an unconventional strategy to curb Nipah and rabies spillovers from bats by using mosquitoes as vaccine carriers. In the approach, Aedes aegypti mosquitoes are fed blood containing Nipah or rabies vaccines so the vaccine disseminates to their salivary glands. When these vaccinated mosquitoes bite bats, antibodies against Nipah and rabies were detected in laboratory tests, and subsequent experiments in mice and bats showed anti-viral antibodies. If scalable, this could prevent bat-to-human transmission, reducing future outbreaks. However, experts warn that controlling which individuals are bitten by mosquitoes is highly challenging, and it remains unclear whether transmission would be truly halted. Alternative methods, such as drinking stations where vaccinated solutions are placed for water-consuming bats, are being explored. The work is in early stages and laboratory-based.",
