        "articleSummary": "Vietnam faces suspected mass banh mi-related food poisoning in Ho Chi Minh City in early March, with about 130 people hospitalised in Hanh Thong and Vung Tau Wards as southern Vietnam enters the hot season. While banh mi itself is rarely the direct cause, issues center on pâté, cold cuts, and Vietnamese mayo, especially large, unrefrigerated pâté blocks (0.5–1 kg) from stalls. Common pathogens include Salmonella and E. coli; symptoms include diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, potentially dehydration and electrolyte imbalance; severe cases can progress to systemic infection or sepsis. Incubation for Salmonella roughly 0.5–72 hours. Contamination can occur via unclean handling, utensils, or unsanitary markets, and heat accelerates spoilage. Health authorities in Ho Chi Minh City are increasing inspections; advice remains to ensure thorough cooking and avoid raw/undercooked dishes during hot weather.",
