        "articleSummary": "Leprosy (Hansen’s disease) is depicted through Juan Campos’s life from Málaga, diagnosed in 1962 and interned at Trillo leprosery in Guadalajara, facing extreme stigma and family rejection. By 1968 he could return as antibiotics reduced contagion; in 1981 a three-drug multidrug therapy cured the disease, with WHO providing free MDT since then. Campos’s later health deteriorated, culminating in suicide at 60 in 1993. Lola Campos has published Nuestro Dios privado, drawing on Fontilles in Alicante—the last European leprosy sanatorium and a global reference center—to recount patient testimonies and the quiet, enduring mistreatment of individuals with leprosy in Spain. Spain reports 10–15 new cases per year, while leprosy remains ongoing globally, especially in India.",
