        "articleSummary": "Avian influenza H5N1 has ripped through poultry farms, killing tens of millions of chickens and turkeys, and recently spilled into dairy cattle, while human cases remain very rare as of March 2026. Although avian flu can enter human cells, a multi-layer cellular defense blocks sustained spread. A DNA-sensing STING pathway triggers NF-κB–driven antiviral responses; GADD34, a key gene activated by STING, helps limit replication. In experiments with lab mice and human lung cells, removing STING or GADD34 leads to higher viral levels and more severe disease, underscoring STING as an important barrier. The avian M1 matrix protein, by suppressing STING signaling, appears to be chosen in many human pandemic strains—over 95% carry this mutation. This points to multiple checkpoints beyond receptor binding that the virus must clear to spill over, with potential therapeutic targeting of STING-M1 interactions. Research reported from Zhejiang University, China, and published in Science.",
        "articleId": "aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cucG5hcy5vcmcvcG9zdC9qb3VybmFsLWNsdWIvYmlyZC1mbHUtY2FuLWFscmVhZHktZ2V0LWluc2lkZS1odW1hbi1jZWxscy1zby13aHktaGFzbi10LXNwYXJrZWQtcGFuZGVtaWM="
