        "articleSummary": "Measles remains a deadly, highly contagious viral disease despite vaccines. In 2018, more than 140,000 people died, predominantly in low-income countries with weak health systems. Top outbreak countries include Nigeria, India, Pakistan, Liberia, Ethiopia, Afghanistan, DRC, Indonesia and Ivory Coast. Zimbabwe reports 2,056 cumulative measles cases through Aug 15, with at least 157 deaths since April 2022. The World Health Organization warned Africa of a surge in preventable diseases due to vaccination delays; measles cases in Africa jumped by at least 400%. Measles presents with high fever, cough, runny nose, red watery eyes, and a rash; Koplik spots may appear 2–3 days after symptoms. Incubation 7–14 days. Children under five (and those over 20) are high risk. Unvaccinated children remain vulnerable; barriers include religious sects like Apostolic groups. Recommended actions: accelerate vaccination, improve vaccine supply/storage/transport, engage faith/traditional leaders, potentially mandating vaccination; robust case management.",
