        "articleSummary": "Chikungunya resurges with a more aggressive variant, as reported by Dr. Sumit Rawat of Sagar Bundelkhand Medical College, in collaboration with AIIMS Bhopal. Drawing on India's 2006 nationwide outbreak and subsequent spikes in 2016 and 2024, the study identifies a new chikungunya variant associated with more severe disease. Infected patients may develop brain fever (encephalitis), heart inflammation, lung inflammation, immune suppression, dizziness, and prolonged joint pain. The findings originate from Sagar, Madhya Pradesh, with local coverage by 18 News, highlighting potential higher morbidity and the need for awareness and preventive measures. The report does not specify laboratory confirmation details or infection sources. Keywords: chikungunya, outbreak, Madhya Pradesh, encephalitis, myocarditis, pneumonitis, joint pain, coronavirus-free (not mentioned).",
