        "articleSummary": "As of 15 March 2026, Makassar, South Sulawesi, reports a measles surge with 187 suspected cases and 2 laboratory-confirmed positives; hundreds of specimens are pending. Makassar is among 10 Indonesian districts/cities with a measles KLB for two consecutive years (2025 and 2026), after 183 cases in 2025 in Makassar and 10 in Sinjai. The most affected are children 6 months–5 years, especially 9–24 months, often not fully immunized. Morbillivirus is highly contagious via droplets and can persist in air or on surfaces for up to two hours, with one case exposing about 12–18 others. Complications include pneumonia, diarrhea, encephalitis, otitis media, and malnutrition, with higher mortality in malnourished or immunocompromised children. Health authorities launched Outbreak Response Immunization in Makassar and Sinjai, while routine MR immunization (9 months, 18 months, and first grade) remains in effect. Seek care for fever, cough, runny nose, red eyes, and rash.",
