        "articleSummary": "Lenacapavir (Yeztugo), a long-acting injectable HIV prevention drug, yields rare breakthrough infections reported at CROI 2026 in Denver. In PURPOSE I, >5,300 cisgender adolescent girls and young women in sub-Saharan Africa; among >2,000 injections, 2 HIV infections vs 77 in daily PrEP. In PURPOSE II, >3,000 men who have sex with men and gender-diverse individuals; 3 infections with Lenacapavir vs 12 on daily pills. Breakthrough infection rate ~0.07–0.11 per 100 person-years. Four of five breakthrough cases showed resistance mutations; researchers explore biological/pharmacokinetic explanations. Cost exceeds $28,000/year in the US, raising access concerns. Rollouts began February 2026 in Zimbabwe, South Africa, Zambia, Malawi, with Zimbabwe piloting February 19 in Epworth and ~46,000 doses; focus on 24 high-risk locations and groups such as adolescent girls and young women, sex workers, pregnant/breastfeeding women. Despite rare infections, protection nears 99%.",
