        "articleSummary": "Human metapneumovirus (HMPV) is a respiratory virus in Pneumoviridae, closely related to RSV. First identified in the Netherlands in 2001, it now infects nearly all children by age five and circulates worldwide. In temperate regions, infections peak in winter and early spring; in tropical areas, transmission occurs year-round. HMPV spreads via respiratory droplets and direct contact, with a 3–6 day incubation. Symptoms mimic cold, influenza, or RSV infections and include fever, cough, runny nose, sore throat, and fatigue; severe cases may cause wheezing, shortness of breath, bronchitis, or pneumonia. Healthy adults usually have mild illness, but children <5 and adults ≥65, especially with chronic or immunosuppressive conditions, are at higher risk of hospitalization. No antiviral or vaccine exists; treatment is supportive. Prevention relies on hand hygiene and staying home when ill. Recent reports from North America, Asia, and China indicate substantial HMPV activity, sometimes exceeding seasonal influenza counts, with under-testing.",
