        "articleSummary": "Brazilian health and environmental authorities are monitoring the invasive African snail, Lissachatina fulica, across multiple regions due to environmental, agricultural, and public health risks. Originating in East Africa, it was introduced in the 1980s for snail farming; after farming failed, many specimens were released, enabling rapid spread. The species thrives in vacant lots, backyards, gardens, and debris and reproduces prolifically, laying hundreds of eggs. Besides ecological and crop damage, it can harbor parasites that cause human disease, including eosinophilic meningitis and abdominal angiostrongyliasis. Public guidance includes avoiding direct contact with the snail; if handling is necessary, wear gloves or use plastic bags and maintain clean, debris-free areas to curb proliferation. Monitoring is ongoing across Brazil as of 15 March 2026.",
        "articleId": "aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cub21haXNwb3NpdGl2by5jb20uYnIvY2FyYW11am8tYWZyaWNhbm8tcHJlb2N1cGEtYXV0b3JpZGFkZXMtZGUtc2F1ZGUtZS1tZWlvLWFtYmllbnRlLw=="
