        "articleSummary": "Chikungunya vaccination campaign in Mirassol, Brazil, began February 2, with uptake lagging: fewer than 3,000 of a 37,000-person target (ages 18–59). Following Ministry of Health guidance (Nota Técnica 30/2026), Mirassol allows residents from other municipalities to be vaccinated if doses are available, reflecting regional mobility. The vaccine, developed by Valneva with Instituto Butantan and Funfarme, was approved by Anvisa in April 2025 and is deployed strategically in selected municipalities based on epidemiology, population size, and health network capacity. In 2024 Brazil reported 263,502 chikungunya cases and 246 deaths, underscoring vaccination importance. Mirassol posts operate Monday–Friday, 7:00–17:00; photo ID required. Eligible adults: 18–59; travelers to outbreak areas; not recommended for pregnant/nursing; immunocompromised; uncontrolled comorbidities; allergy to vaccine components. Disease is transmitted by Aedes aegypti; symptoms include fever, severe joint pain, headache, rash; complications can include chronic arthralgia.",
