        "articleSummary": "As of 2026-03-15, Thailand reports confirmed cases of Severe Fever with Thrombocytopenia Syndrome (SFTS) caused by SFTSV transmitted by ticks and through contact with animals. The Institute of Neurology, Ministry of Public Health, confirmed the risk following verification by Thailand's Fake News Center that tick bites can cause encephalitis from SFTSV. Typical presentations include high fever, thrombocytopenia (low platelets), and elevated liver enzymes; some patients develop severe complications such as multi-organ failure or meningoencephalitis, which can be life-threatening. The case fatality rate among reported cases ranges from 5% to 28%. Transmission occurs via tick bites; not all tick exposures lead to severe disease. Ongoing awareness in Thailand about tick exposure and potential neurological complications is noted.",
