        "articleSummary": "Chikungunya outbreak in Dourados, Mato Grosso do Sul, Brazil prompts a federal response as the Ministry of Health forms a task force to combat the disease in indigenous villages about 250 km from Campo Grande. There are 150 confirmed chikungunya cases and one death of an indigenous woman in the municipality. On March 7 a cleanup was conducted, and from March 9–11 health teams treated 1,156 homes. Vector control teams found Aedes aegypti breeding sites in 589 residences (26% of visits), mainly in water tanks, garbage, and tires. The municipality argues that mosquito control and village health are federal responsibilities, while the Ministry says it is monitoring the Dourados Indigenous Reserve, installing ovitrampas (egg traps) and conducting health education. The task force will intensify measures to eliminate breeding sites and apply insecticides, with multiple agencies involved.",
