        "articleSummary": "Mpox (monkeypox) spreads in Brazil, with urgent public guidance from the Ministry of Health. By March 2026, Brazil records about 88 confirmed mpox cases across multiple states, concentrated in large urban centers led by São Paulo; other affected states include Rio de Janeiro, Minas Gerais, Rondônia, Rio Grande do Sul, Paraná, and the Distrito Federal. No deaths have been reported in 2026 to date. Transmission occurs mainly via close contact with infected people, especially through skin lesions, bodily secretions, or prolonged respiratory droplets; contaminated objects can also transmit the virus, and intimate contact (including sex) can facilitate spread. Symptoms include fever, body aches, swollen lymph nodes, and skin lesions evolving into blisters and crusts. The SUS remains prepared to diagnose and monitor suspected cases, with ongoing surveillance, guidance for health professionals, and public information campaigns. Key measures: avoid contact with lesions or contaminated objects, seek medical care for fever with rash, maintain hand hygiene, isolate suspected cases, and consult official sources; vaccination may be indicated for some priority groups.",
