        "articleSummary": "Guangdong CDC warns that dengue fever and chikungunya fever may enter active transmission as warm weather returns (Mar 15–17) in Guangdong, driven by Aedes mosquitoes. No case counts are provided. Travelers returning from Southeast Asia or South America should monitor for symptoms within 14 days of return. Early signs include fever; dengue may cause rapid high fever (≥39°C within 24 hours); chikungunya commonly presents with severe joint pain lasting weeks to months; rashes may occur after fever. If suspected, seek prompt medical care at fever clinics or emergency departments and inform clinicians of recent mosquito bites or travel history. Do not self-medicate with NSAIDs (ibuprofen, aspirin) before diagnosis due to bleeding risk. Prevent by removing standing water, ensuring screens or air conditioning, using insecticides, wearing light-colored long clothing, and applying DEET-containing repellents. Returned travelers with symptoms should seek care quickly.",
