        "articleSummary": "Deforestation-driven biodiversity loss in Brazil's Atlantic Forest is pushing some mosquito species to rely more on human blood, increasing potential disease transmission to nearby populations. A study by Oswaldo Cruz Institute (Rio de Janeiro) and the Federal University of Rio de Janeiro, conducted in two protected reserves in Rio de Janeiro State, collected mosquitoes with light traps and used laboratory DNA analysis of blood meals to identify feeding sources. Results show a large share of meals from humans rather than wildlife, correlating with reduced biodiversity due to habitat destruction. The feeding shift could elevate transmission risk of arboviruses such as yellow fever, dengue, Zika, and chikungunya. Researchers emphasize that preserving ecosystem diversity and environmental protections may mitigate these risks in the future.",
        "articleId": "aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cueW91bTcuY29tL3N0b3J5LzIwMjYvMy8xNS-Yqtiq2LrYsNmJLdi52YTZiS3Yr9mF2KfYoS3Yp9mE2KjYtNixLdin2YTYqNi52YjYti3ZitmH2K-Yry3Yp9mE2YXZhNin2YrZitmGLdio2LPYqNioLdiq2K-ZhdmK2LEt2LrYp9io2KfYqi83MzQyMDc1"
      },
