        "articleSummary": "Yellow fever resurges in the Americas, with the World Health Organization urging enhanced prevention and surveillance. In 2025, 346 human cases and 143 deaths were reported across seven countries, a 5.6-fold rise from 2024’s 61 cases. In weeks 1–7 of 2026 (Jan–Feb), 34 cases and 15 deaths were recorded regionwide. Brazil leads current totals (120 cases, 48 deaths), followed by Colombia (125 cases, 46 deaths); Bolivia has 1 death, Peru 2 cases, and Venezuela 6 cases (1 death). Colombia alone reported 25 cases (including 13 deaths) in weeks 1–7, with Venezuela, Peru and Bolivia contributing additional cases. Cases have begun appearing outside the Amazon basin, signaling geographic spread beyond traditional foci. Yellow fever is a mosquito-borne virus; severe illness can cause liver and kidney failure and severe bleeding, with up to ~50% case fatality. Vaccination, strengthened surveillance, and preventive measures are urgently urged.",
