        "articleSummary": "Measles outbreaks intensify across Indonesia in 2025–early 2026 amid antivaccine debates. The Health Ministry urged families to delay homecoming travel if children show measles symptoms. In 2025, Indonesia recorded 63,769 suspected measles cases, 11,094 laboratory-confirmed cases, and 69 deaths, with suspected cases rising about 147% from the previous year. In early 2026, the Indonesian Pediatric Society (IDAI) reported 379 confirmed measles cases in the first five weeks, while suspected cases remained in the thousands and laboratories were backlogged. There were 45 measles outbreaks (KLB) across 29 districts in 11 provinces. The report notes severe pediatric illness, including bronkopneumonia/pneumonia and high fever with rapid breathing; a 7-month-old infant who could not be vaccinated was affected. Public debate targets antivaccine parents; calls for more voices from vaccinated mothers. A boundary-setting approach—excluding unvaccinated children from playgroups—is proposed to shield others.",
