        "articleSummary": "West Nile fever circulates in Brazil, per a SBMT bulletin shared by Tião Viana, physician and former governor of Acre. The virus shows evidence of circulation in multiple Brazilian regions over the past decade, with 110 probable or confirmed cases reported in 13 states from 2014–2024, indicating ongoing activity but a low rate of clinical diagnoses. A recent confirmed case in Mato Grosso involving a baby rekindled debate about the virus’s presence in the country. West Nile fever is an arbovirus transmitted mainly by Culex mosquitoes, with birds as natural reservoirs; humans and other mammals are incidental hosts. About 80% of infections are asymptomatic; others present with fever and body pains, and rare cases may involve neurological complications. The SBMT calls for continuous surveillance and mosquito-control measures, emphasizing vigilance rather than an outbreak.",
