        "articleSummary": "A Guangdong man from Shanwei drank raw pig blood and, six months later, developed seizures, foaming at the mouth, and loss of consciousness. Brain MRI initially raised concern for a tumor, but after excluding neoplasms and vascular disease and considering dietary exposure, doctors diagnosed cerebral neurocysticercosis caused by Taenia solium cysticerci entering the central nervous system via bloodstream. The patient, in his 30s and who had worked in Zhejiang, underwent workup in Guangzhou and Guangzhou before treatment in Hangzhou. At Shulan Hospital (Hangzhou) Infectious Diseases Department, led by Dr. Gao Haina, his right frontal lobe lesion and intracranial edema progressively shrank with targeted therapy over six months, with symptoms resolving. A follow-up in March 2026 confirmed improvement. He was advised to avoid raw pig blood, highlighting the infection risk from undercooked pork products and the lack of an outbreak-like situation.",
