        "articleSummary": "Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SRAG) cases are rising across Brazil, per Fiocruz InfoGripe. In Epidemiological Week 8 (Feb 22–28), increases occurred in 23 states and the Federal District; Roraima, Tocantins, Espírito Santo and Rio Grande do Sul did not rise. Hospitalizations have grown, with a notable rise among children and adolescents—linked to the return to school. Parents are advised to keep symptomatic kids at home or, if needed, have them wear masks in class. Ten states report SRAG at alert/risk/high risk; 12 of 27 capitals show activity with a long-term growth trend. In 2026, over 14,300 SRAG cases were notified, with 35% lab-confirmed for a respiratory virus; rhinovirus is most detected, followed by influenza A and SARS-CoV-2. COVID-19 accounts for most deaths, then influenza A and rhinovirus. Incidence remains higher in young children, while mortality is highest among the elderly.",
