        "articleSummary": "First identified in Uruguay, Sporothrix brasiliensis, a fungus causing sporotrichosis, now poses a cat-to-human transmission risk with alerts for potential epidemics. The finding arose from a 2025 intrafamilial outbreak affecting people and their pets; initial link to a Brazilian street cat was later disproven as cases appeared in other areas. Dr. Elisa Cabeza notes the discovery confirms fungal circulation in Uruguay and supports strengthened surveillance, especially for vulnerable populations such as children under two and older adults. Sporotrichosis transmits from infected cats to humans when the fungus enters the skin via scratches, bites, or contact with wounds or secretions. Symptoms typically appear days to weeks after exposure: a small skin lesion at the injury site, followed by nodules along lymphatic pathways, with inflammation or redness. If diagnosed early, antifungal treatment is effective; veterinarians advise owners to inspect cats’ muzzles and ears for wounds or discharge.",
