        "articleSummary": "Sporotrichosis, a fungal infection primarily affecting the skin and subcutaneous tissue, is spreading from cats in South America. In late February, the Instituto de Higiene issued a population warning as surveillance in the Southern Cone intensified; Argentina also reports rising cases. The World Health Organization notes this Sporothrix fungus causes zoonotic infections transmitted via cats, with more than 11,000 human cases reported in the affected areas of South America over the past decade, mainly in Brazil. Transmission can also occur from other mammals or through contaminated materials or vegetation. Most infections are cutaneous, but rare cases can involve meningitis, arthritis, or respiratory infections. The alerts reflect regional monitoring changes and cross-border risk, with outbreaks linked to wood, mining materials, or flood-affected environments.",
