        "articleSummary": "Measles outbreaks are rising in Japan, with Tokyo and Osaka reporting the most cases this year. Taiwan CDC reports four domestic measles cases by March 4: two locally acquired and two imported from Vietnam and Malaysia. Early measles symptoms—fever, cough, runny nose, and red eyes—mimic a cold, though contagiousness is high before the rash appears. The surge underscores waning MMR vaccination coverage and the risk of community transmission resurfacing in clinics and hospitals. Authorities urge catch-up vaccination to strengthen herd protection and remind travelers to seek boosters if immunity is uncertain. Key vaccination targets: adults born 1981–2000 (ages 20–45) due to waning protection; healthcare workers, aviation/travel personnel, and the immunocompromised who should check protection, and, if no record in 15 years, receive a booster. Infants under 1 aren’t covered by public MMR, but a paid dose at age 6 months is available for those traveling to outbreak zones.",
