        "articleSummary": "Sporothrix brasiliensis, a fungus affecting cats, is causing a cutaneous esporotricosis outbreak in southern Uruguay, around Maldonado and Rocha. Originating in Brazil, authorities warned in late February about the spread, with Argentina also reporting rising cases, signaling a Southern Cone shift in surveillance. The fungus thrives in tropical/subtropical climates and can be transmitted to humans via contact with infected felines, leading to esporotricosis that penetrates the skin and subcutaneous tissue; rare complications include meningitis, arthritis, or respiratory infections. In affected South American regions, Brazil has documented more than 11,000 human cases in the past decade. Diagnosis uses microscopy, histopathology, or culture, and treatment is straightforward and effective. Primary symptoms after feline contact are skin eruptions or red patches. Transmission can also occur from other mammals or contaminated materials/vegetation, with outbreaks worldwide linked to wood, mining materials, or floods.",
