        "articleSummary": "West Nile virus risk in Seville informs a municipal action map under the Junta de Andalucía vector surveillance program. Thirteen large water-associated enclaves will be the larval-control core, using Bacillus thuringiensis israelensis in ponds, lagoons and unchlorinated sources; corrective actions include drainage, drying of water accumulations, drain cleaning, irrigation control to prevent pooling, silicon films on small water surfaces, and selective chitin inhibitors. Adulticidal chemicals are reserved as a last resort in confirmed risk. The plan also covers other breeding-prone environments such as construction sites, abandoned lots, sewers, industrial zones, pools, schools, cemeteries, municipal facilities, water infrastructure, port and military areas, and busy sites like the Real Alcázar and Palacio de Congresos. Sampling points include CD San Pablo, CEPER Polígono Sur and EDAR Tablada exterior. The plan is adaptable to surveillance results and health authority guidance. Last season: 263 lots analyzed, over 3,500 potential vectors screened with no West Nile detected; 1,667 locations inspected with localized actions as needed.",
