        "articleSummary": "Philippines imposes a moratorium on importing poultry, domestic and wild birds and by-products from Indiana due to avian influenza outbreaks. The Department of Agriculture bans shipments of poultry meat, day-old chicks, eggs, and semen from the US Midwest state after confirmed multiple outbreaks of the highly pathogenic H5N1 avian influenza, per the February 23, 2026 APHIS report with laboratory confirmation by the National Veterinary Services Laboratories in Ames, Iowa. The move aims to shield the multibillion-peso Philippine poultry industry from exposure amid the rapid spread of the virus across the United States. Under a 2016 bilateral animal health arrangement, a state-wide ban may be imposed if three or more counties are affected; Indiana reportedly breached this threshold per World Organisation for Animal Health (WOAH) reports. The import ban suspends SPSIC processing; previously approved SPS clearances for live birds are revoked. Shipments already in transit or accepted before effectivity may enter if slaughtered/produced on or before January 30, with strict port enforcement by veterinary quarantine officers.",
