        "articleSummary": "China-based researchers led by Aihua Zheng from the Chinese Academy of Sciences have developed a novel method to vaccinate bats against deadly viruses, including Nipah and rabies, aiming to reduce spillover to humans. The approach abandons culling in favor of immunizing the bat population. It uses genetically modified mosquitoes as vaccine carriers: mosquitoes are fed blood enriched with a modified vaccine and can transmit protection to bats by bite or when bats eat the mosquitoes. An additional oral vaccine solution administered through drinking water has also shown efficacy in protecting bats from infection. Early tests indicate exposed bats develop antibodies against the viruses, signaling an immune response. If scalable to wild populations, this could lower human infection risk in regions where bat-borne viruses threaten public health. Researchers seek international collaboration to adapt the methods to different environments and assess long-term effectiveness.",
