        "articleSummary": "Pulmonary tuberculosis remains a major infectious disease in Hong Kong, with annual reported TB cases averaging 3,000–4,500 over the past decade and incidence about tenfold higher than in other advanced economies. Health Chief Lam Man-kin, speaking at World Tuberculosis Day 2026, warned that approximately one in twenty Hong Kong residents will contract TB if unchecked. Smoking is a significant risk factor for TB infection and relapse. In response, the government plans to expand smoke-free zones, ban designated alternative smoking products in public places, implement a tax labeling system, prohibit flavored traditional tobacco, and harmonize packaging for traditional products. The Department of Health’s Chest and Lung Services monitors TB control, promotes universal Bacillus Calmette-Guérin vaccination for newborns, and provides free TB screening and treatment at chest clinics. Public education campaigns will raise awareness and encourage timely diagnosis and treatment to end TB.",
