        "articleSummary": "EU regulators issued a positive scientific opinion for acoziborole on 27 February 2026 under the EU-M4all pathway, paving national approvals in countries where gambiense sleeping sickness remains endemic. The three-tablet, single-dose oral treatment targets adults and adolescents ≥12 years and ≥40 kg, enabling diagnosis and cure in a single visit. Phase II/III data from the Democratic Republic of Congo and Guinea (208 patients, 18 months) show about 95% cure in late-stage disease and 100% in early-stage cases. Sleeping sickness (human African trypanosomiasis) is transmitted by tsetse flies; if untreated, CNS infection is fatal. The disease burden has fallen from >27,000 cases in 1999 to 546 in Africa in 2024 (WHO), with gambiense accounting for >90% of infections in West and Central Africa, where humans are the main reservoir. Acoziborole follows NECT (2009) and fexinidazole (2018), and could streamline delivery in remote settings, accelerating elimination.",
        "articleId": "aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuaW5kZXBlbmRlbnQuY28udWcvYWZyaWNhcy1vbmUtZG9zZS1jdXJlLWJyaW5ncy1zbGVlcGluZy1zaWNrbmVzcy1lbGltaW5hdGlvbi13aXRoaW4tcmVhY2gv"
      },
