        "articleSummary": "Measles outbreaks reported in Dompu Regency, West Nusa Tenggara (NTB), Indonesia, driven by incomplete measles-rubella (MR) vaccination during the COVID-19 pandemic. Most cases occur in children under five, particularly those born in 2021–2022 when activity restrictions and suspension of posyandu community health posts limited immunization. Dinas Kesehatan Dompu director Mariam Ulfa notes suspected measles cases are in their ninth week, though the rate is lower than in week eight. The NTB provincial government had declared a measles outbreak (KLB) in Bima Regency, Bima City, and Dompu; Dompu has not formally declared KLB but is applying similar outbreak responses, including ORI (Outbreak Response Immunization) campaigns. As of week 7, 2026, 985 suspected measles cases have been recorded across the three districts. Health officials cite incomplete MR coverage, fluctuations in routine immunization, high mobility, and delays in detection and early response as key factors.",
