        "articleSummary": "Measles cases rise in the Yogyakarta Special Region (DIY), Indonesia, with hundreds of suspected cases and dozens laboratory-confirmed in early 2026, including in Yogyakarta City and Sleman Regency. The uptick is driven by gaps in immunization, ongoing delays in routine childhood vaccination during the COVID-19 pandemic, and parental doubts about vaccine safety and efficacy. Symptoms begin with high fever, cough, runny nose, and red eyes, followed days later by a rash starting on the face and spreading body-wide. Without proper care, measles can cause severe complications such as respiratory infections, dehydration, diarrhea, and immune system impairment, especially in infants and immunocompromised children. Vaccination with MR or MMR, administered on schedule, remains the most effective prevention. Public health messaging and education through clinics, hospitals, posyandu, and schools are urged to address misinformation and close immunization gaps.",
