        "articleSummary": "Measles cases in South Sumatra are rising in 2026, with 1,082 suspected and 184 confirmed by week 9, up from 744 suspected and 68 confirmed in 2025. January 2026 saw 381 suspected with 151 positives; February had 525 suspected; March 2026 had 176 suspected and 33 positives. Authorities cite low immunization coverage as a key risk factor. To curb transmission, Outbreak Response Immunization (ORI) was launched on March 10 in high-risk districts Palembang, Prabumulih, Banyuasin, and Musi Rawas Utara, delivered via posyandu and health facilities. Officials Ira Primadesa (P2P, Dinkes Sumsel) and Trisnawarman (Dinkes Sumsel) warn residents to seek testing for measles symptoms: high fever lasting several days with red spots on the skin. The public is urged to ensure children complete immunization, as vaccination is the most effective prevention.",
