        "articleSummary": "Bird flu outbreak in Indiana prompts Philippines import ban. The US Department of Agriculture APHIS confirmed multiple avian influenza cases in Indiana, with laboratory confirmation by the National Veterinary Services Laboratories in Ames, Iowa. In response, the Philippines Department of Agriculture orders an immediate suspension of shipments involving domestic and wild birds and related products from Indiana, including poultry meat, day-old chicks, eggs and poultry semen, and halts processing and issuance of sanitary and phytosanitary import clearances for these commodities. The measure aims to reduce exposure risk as the virus spreads across parts of the United States. Under a 2016 animal health agreement with the United States, statewide import restrictions can be imposed when at least three counties are affected; Indiana has surpassed that threshold. Previously issued SPS clearances for live birds are revoked. Shipments already in transit or accepted at port before the directive may enter if slaughtered/produced on or before 30 January.",
