        "articleSummary": "Argentina’s CAPIA calls for immediate mass vaccination against Highly Pathogenic Avian Influenza (HPAI) after outbreaks in commercial facilities in Buenos Aires and Córdoba and the third loss of sanitary status with OMSA. CAPIA argues the non-vaccination strategy is obsolete and endangers the domestic market (about 94.75% of production for internal consumption) to protect exports (about 5.25%). The group notes that the EU, the Americas, and China vaccinate to protect poultry, while Argentina leaves producers exposed. Outbreaks have led to export suspensions of chickens and eggs to several countries; the eggs sector is deemed higher risk due to its concentrated structure. Under Senasa protocols, an outbreak triggers total culling, loss of working capital without indemnification, and mass layoffs. CAPIA proposes vaccination of long-cycle birds and urges the national government to prioritize animal health to safeguard protein supply for Argentinians.",
