        "articleSummary": "Hong Kong records about 3,000–4,500 pulmonary tuberculosis cases annually, more than ten times the incidence in other advanced economies. Over a lifetime, roughly 1 in 20 residents will develop TB. At World TB Day 2026 opening ceremony, the Secretary for Health attended and noted TB remains a major infectious disease in Hong Kong; smoking is a key risk factor for infection and relapse. TB notification has fallen from about 150 per 100,000 in the early 1980s to around 40 per 100,000 today. The Department of Health, via Chest and Lung Services, monitors and controls TB, promotes universal newborn BCG vaccination, and provides free TB screening and treatment at chest clinics. Public health outreach includes online and social-media campaigns and educational talks aiming to end TB in Hong Kong.",
