        "articleSummary": "Mirzapur, Uttar Pradesh: A 14-year-old boy from Kachhwa, named Karan, develops rabies four months after a stray-dog bite. He now shows hydrophobia and barking-like vocalizations. Delay in reporting the bite led to postponed post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP), allowing the virus to progress to an incurable stage. Rabies can incubate for weeks to over a year, and once furious rabies symptoms appear, survival is virtually impossible. At presentation, clinicians are providing palliative care. The Mirzapur Chief Medical Officer urged all close contacts exposed to the boy's saliva or wounds to receive anti-rabies vaccine. WHO guidance recommends washing any bite wound with soap and water for at least 15 minutes, followed by a full course of ARV. This case highlights the fatal risk of rabies and the need for immediate action after animal bites.",
