        "articleSummary": "On World Tuberculosis Day 2026 (March 14), Hong Kong confirms that TB remains a major infectious disease, though the notification rate has fallen by over 70% across 40 years. Local TB notifications over the past decade run 3,000–4,500 per year, with incidence now about 40 per 100,000, roughly ten times higher than in many advanced economies. Officials warn that TB still affects the population substantially, with about one in twenty Hong Kong residents potentially developing TB. Smoking is a major risk factor. Measures include expanding smoke-free zones, banning possession of designated alternative smoking products in public, implementing a tax-labeling regime, banning flavored traditional cigarettes, and unifying packaging for traditional smoking products. The Department of Health promotes universal BCG vaccination for newborns, TB education, and provides free TB screening and treatment at Chest and Lung Clinics, alongside health education talks to end TB.",
        "articleId": "aHR0cDovL2hrY25hLmhrL2RvY0RldGFpbC5qc3A-aWQ9MTAxMjY5NjQ5JmNoYW5uZWw9MjgxMw=="
