        "articleSummary": "Measles cases surge in Palembang, Indonesia, with 65 laboratory-confirmed cases in 2026 as of March 12 and 456 suspected nationwide, driven by low vaccination coverage. Palembang Health Department will provide supplementary measles vaccination for children aged 9 and 18 months and for 1st-grade students. An Outbreak Response Immunization (ORI) campaign is underway in March 2026, vaccinating all children aged 9–59 months regardless of previous immunization; if target coverage remains below 95%, activities will continue into April. Vaccination sites include Posyandu, TK/PAUD, mudik posts, places of worship, hospitals, clinics, doctor practices, and house-to-house visits if needed. Authorities also advocate Vitamin A, distancing ill children from pregnant women, and adherence to Healthy Living Practices (PHBS). With Eid al-Fitr approaching, vaccination before mudik is urged; masks in crowded indoor spaces, hand hygiene, and attention to severe measles symptoms—fever above 39°C, spreading rash, dyspnea, seizures, diarrhea or vomiting—are advised.",
