        "articleSummary": "Natal, Brazil—Natal's Municipal Health Secretariat confirms the city’s first mpox case in 2026. A 44-year-old man, resident of Natal, was lab-confirmed on February 20; he completed 15 days of home isolation, recovered, and had no further symptoms. By March 14, four mpox notifications were recorded in 2026: one confirmed, two suspected, one discarded. Mpox is caused by mpox virus (MPXV), an Orthopoxvirus in Poxviridae, transmitted by close contact with an infected person, contaminated animals, or materials. Symptoms include fever, headache, body aches, chills, fatigue, lymphadenopathy, and skin lesions that may crust. Diagnosis uses molecular tests or genetic sequencing; patients should isolate during transmission and avoid sharing personal items. Vaccination is available for priority groups—adults over 18 living with HIV/AIDS and healthcare workers directly managing mpox. Since 2022, Natal has 100 confirmed and 176 discarded mpox cases with no mpox-related deaths; surveillance follows Ministry of Health protocols.",
        "articleId": "aHR0cHM6Ly90cmlidW5hZG9ub3J0ZS5jb20uYnIvbmF0YWwvbmF0YWwtY29uZmlybWEtcHJpbWVpcm8tY2Fzby1kZS1tcG94LWVtLTIwMjYtZS1yZWZvcmNhLW9yaWVudGFjb2VzLWRlLXByZXZlbmNhby8="
