        "articleSummary": "Washing raw chicken increases, not reduces, the risk of spreading bacteria in kitchens. Citing USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service and NHS, rinsing raw chicken does not improve safety; running water can cause splashes that spread bacteria to countertops, sinks, utensils, and other foods. Bacteria from raw poultry, especially Campylobacter and sometimes Salmonella, can transfer via hands, clothing, and surfaces. Campylobacter infection typically appears 2–5 days after exposure, with abdominal pain, severe diarrhea, fever, and vomiting; rare complications include irritable bowel syndrome, reactive arthritis, and Guillain‑Barré syndrome. In Indonesia, open-air markets and street vendors contribute to concerns about contamination, prompting washing habits. Safer alternatives include using a basin of water instead of running water, thoroughly drying with paper towels, cleaning surfaces and tools with soap, washing hands after handling, and cooking chicken to doneness with no pink meat and proper internal temperature. The key is cooking, not washing.",
