        "articleSummary": "Brazil faces oral transmission of Chagas disease linked to artisanal açaí, with about 69% of acute Chagas cases (2007–2016) attributed to contaminated foods such as açaí and cane juice. Trypanosoma cruzi inactivate via heat treatment: heat the product in water at 80–90°C for about 10 minutes, then immediately transfer to ambient-temperature water for blanching. During selection, remove stones, leaves, and insects—particularly the triatomine barbeiro—to reduce contamination risk. Acute Chagas commonly presents with fever lasting over seven days, headache, pronounced weakness, facial and leg edema, and a furuncle-like lesion at the entry site; some infections are asymptomatic. Chronic disease may develop years later, often asymptomatic but can cause cardiomyopathy/heart failure and digestive tract changes such as megacolon or megaesophagus. Transmission routes include oral (food/drink), vectorial, transfusion or transplantation, accidental exposure, and vertical mother-to-child transmission. Prevention rests on proper food preparation per Ministry of Health guidance.",
