        "articleSummary": "PAHO issued an epidemiological alert on sustained Yellow Fever transmission in parts of South America during 2026, extending beyond Amazon basin foci to areas such as São Paulo state, Brazil, and Tolima department, Colombia. In 2025, 346 laboratory-confirmed cases and 143 deaths in seven countries, CFR 41%, with Colombia and Brazil hardest hit, followed by Peru and Venezuela. In seven weeks of 2026, 34 human cases and 15 deaths were reported in Bolivia, Colombia, Peru, and Venezuela, prompting continued high risk level in the Americas. Most confirmed patients lacked vaccination; vaccination is the most effective preventive measure. A single dose provides lifelong protection; countries should reach at least 95% coverage in exposed populations to prevent urban transmission via Aedes aegypti. Travelers should be vaccinated at least 10 days before visiting endemic areas. Non-human primate die-off should be monitored as an early signal. PAHO will maintain vaccine stockpiles and technical support.",
