        "articleSummary": "Measles cases in Indonesia’s Yogyakarta Special Region (DIY) have risen, with hundreds of suspected cases and dozens of confirmed infections reported through 2026. In Sleman Regency, 33 children were confirmed positive for measles and four rubella cases from 148 suspected cases during January–February 2026. In Yogyakarta City, by week 8 of February 2026, six confirmed measles cases were found from 42 suspected cases identified through surveillance. Authorities say most positives are in children who have not completed immunization, a situation worsened by uneven vaccine coverage and the postponement of vaccinations during the COVID-19 pandemic. Measles can cause severe complications such as pneumonia, dehydration, diarrhea, and immune impairment, especially in infants. Vaccination with MR (Measles and Rubella) or MM (Measles, Mumps, Rubella) is essential. Public health measures emphasize education at clinics, hospitals, posyandu, and schools; epidemiological investigations are ongoing to identify sources and contacts. All patients received treatment and prevention counseling.",
        "articleId": "aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cudGVtcG8uY28vc2FpbnMveW9neWFrYXJ0YS1tZW5jYXRhdC1wZW5pbmdrYXRhbi1rYXN1cy1wZW55YWtpdC1jYW1wYWstMjEyMjIwMg=="
