        "articleSummary": "New Delhi, India — Severe COVID-19 or influenza infections cause lasting remodeling of lung tissue that raises lung cancer risk. A University of Virginia Health System study, published in Cell, shows that severe viral pneumonia alters the arrangement of lung immune cells, sustaining inflammation that promotes tumor growth. In humans, patients hospitalized for severe COVID-19 had a 1.24-fold higher risk of lung cancer, even without smoking or comorbidities; moderate infection carried lower risk. Rat models recovering from lung infection showed dramatic increases in lung cancer incidence and poorer recovery. The findings underscore the need for vigilant post-infection surveillance and regular lung cancer screening (e.g., CT scans), especially for smokers or those with prior pneumonia. Vaccination against COVID-19 and influenza may mitigate these harmful lung changes. WHO notes lung cancer as a leading worldwide cause of cancer deaths, highlighting the value of early detection.",
        "articleId": "aHR0cHM6Ly9iZW5nYWxpLmFicGxpdmUuY29tL2xpZmVzdHlsZS9oZWFsdGgvbHVuZy1jYW5jZXItcmlza3MtaGlnaGVyLWZvci1zZXZlcmUtY292aWQtZmx1LWNsYWltcy1uZXctbWVkaWNhbC1yZXNlYXJjaC0xMTY5NzMw"
