        "articleSummary": "Unusual and dangerous fungal outbreak transmitted from cats to humans has been detected in Uruguay. An official document from the Instituto de Higiene states the fungus had no prior records in the country, prompting investigations. The event centers on a 2025 intrafamilial cluster in which a whole family was infected by direct transmission from their cats and other pets. Dr. Elisa Cabeza of the University’s Parasitology and Mycology unit said the fungus entered Uruguay via cats from Brazil, but evidence also suggests it had been circulating locally, with feline cases previously reported in Maldonado and Rocha. The infection poses particular risk to the elderly and children under two, though cases are curable with timely care. In humans, the infection typically presents as skin lesions and ulcers; more severe disease can invade the lymphatic system and affect the nose, eyes, or respiratory tract. The excerpt does not detail transmission route.",
