        "articleSummary": "As of March 14, 2026, Yellow fever transmission alerts from PAHO/OPS report sustained spread in parts of South America, with 34 laboratory-confirmed human cases in the first seven weeks of 2026 and 15 deaths, across Bolivia, Colombia, Peru, and Venezuela. Since September 2024, cases have been detected in areas outside traditional Amazon Basin foci, including locations beyond the Amazon region. The Pan American Health Organization urged Member States to strengthen epidemiological surveillance and intensify vaccination campaigns in at-risk populations, and to ensure travelers to areas where vaccination is recommended are informed and protected. In 2025, 346 confirmed yellow fever cases with 143 deaths were reported across seven countries: Bolivia (8/2), Brazil (120/48), Colombia (125/46), Ecuador (11/8), Guyana, Peru (49/19), and Venezuela (32/19). Yellow fever is a viral disease transmitted by mosquitoes; there is no specific treatment, while vaccination remains the most effective preventive measure.",
