        "articleSummary": "Chikungunya transmission dynamics are modeled with a compartmental framework that includes asymptomatic and symptomatic infectious classes and a media-awareness component. The analysis confirms positivity, boundedness, and stability; the basic reproduction number is derived via the next-generation matrix. Calibration uses cumulative case data from India and high-burden states Gujarat, Karnataka, and Maharashtra, with parameter estimation highlighting mosquito biting rate and infection probabilities as key determinants of epidemic persistence. Contour analyses illustrate threshold behaviors across parameter pairs. A two-time-dependent control framework—reducing human–mosquito contact and enhancing treatment—is studied via Pontryagin's Maximum Principle. Results indicate that simultaneous implementation of both controls yields the greatest reduction in symptomatic infections, while individual controls also provide substantial benefits. Findings underscore the importance of preventive measures, media-driven awareness, and improved treatment in region-specific chikungunya management in India.",
        "articleId": "aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cubmF0dXJlLmNvbS9hcnRpY2xlcy9zNDE1OTgtMDI2LTQwNjgxLTY="
