        "articleSummary": "Abre Campo, Minas Gerais, Brazil, conducted on March 10 its third drone overflight under the TechDengue project to identify Aedes aegypti breeding sites and dengue transmission risks. Drone flights map hard-to-access areas for standing water, enabling Endemic Disease Control Agents to target inspections and interventions more efficiently. The initiative has shifted from a one-off activity to part of routine municipal surveillance, strengthening the city's dengue prevention and response capacity. Authorities stress that community participation remains essential; residents should eliminate water-collecting containers such as tires, plant pots, clogged gutters, and open packaging to reduce mosquito breeding. No dengue case counts are reported in the article; emphasis is on preventive surveillance and coordinated vector-control efforts.",
