        "articleSummary": "Sporothrix brasiliensis, a dimorphic fungus sensitive to heat, has emerged in Uruguay’s Maldonado and Rocha departments, prompting epidemiological surveillance in the Southern Cone. The organism is spreading locally via cats—both pets and street animals—to humans. The Brazilian variant thrives in urban felines, increasing exposure through skin-to-skin contact with infected animals whose wounds and exudates carry spores. In humans, infection usually starts as red, persistent bumps that may ulcerate and form linear avenues along lymphatic vessels in the arms or legs. In cats, signs include lesions; without timely treatment, the fungus can reach organs such as lungs, bones, or, rarely, the brain, with higher risk for immunocompromised people. Prevention emphasizes gloves when handling affected cats and rapid veterinary care; the infection is curable but requires prolonged treatment.",
        "articleId": "aHR0cHM6Ly9lbGNvbWVyY2lvLnBlL3Jlc3B1ZXN0YXMvdHJlbmRzL2VzLXBlbGlncm9zby1lbC1ob25nby1xdWUtcHVlZGUtdHJhbnNtaXRpcnNlLWRlLWdhdG9zLWEtaHVtYW5vcy15LXF1ZS1lc3RhLWRhbmRvLXF1ZS1oYWJsYXItZW4tdXJ1Z3VheS10ZHBlLW5vdGljaWEv"
