        "articleSummary": "Pan American Health Organization (OPS) issues an epidemiological alert for sustained yellow fever transmission across several South American countries, including Bolivia, Brazil, Colombia, Ecuador, Guyana, Peru, and Venezuela. Transmission continues in 2026 and has expanded beyond traditional Amazon basin foci, with cases reported in areas previously not recorded since September 2024. In 2025, 346 yellow fever cases and 143 deaths were confirmed across the region. In the first weeks of 2026, 34 human cases and 15 deaths have been registered in Bolivia, Colombia, Peru, and Venezuela, keeping the alert active. OPS urges strengthened epidemiologic surveillance and expanded vaccination campaigns, especially for high-risk populations, and calls to bolster health systems for early detection and timely management of severe cases, while maintaining strategic vaccine stocks. Yellow fever is mosquito-borne, can cause severe illness; there is no specific treatment, vaccines remain the best preventive measure.",
