        "articleSummary": "Kolkata, 14 March 2026 — Thousands across the city are grappling with fever and sore throat, with many developing a dry cough and choked voice. Doctors attribute a spike in upper-respiratory infections to Adenovirus, rhinovirus, metapneumovirus, and enterovirus circulating during a period of fluctuating temperatures. While pneumonia cases declined as the temperature rose, day–night swings appear to have unleashed multiple viruses, causing persistent coughs lasting up to about a month. Manipal Hospitals report surges in patients with lingering cough; clinicians say adenovirus is likely involved, affecting both children and adults and causing airway inflammation. At Charnock Hospital, six in ten OPD patients had a persistent cough after fever, described as a post-viral cough from epithelial damage in the lungs. Management remains symptomatic; some experts recommend anti-allergic medicines if cough persists beyond a fortnight. A fresh uptick in hoarseness and voice loss linked to viral infections was noted at Manipal Broadway Hospital.",
