        "articleSummary": "Viral diseases are rising in Bhopal, Madhya Pradesh, during spring, according to Dr. Ajay Gupta of Khan Shakir Ali Hospital. Influenza A, dengue, hepatitis A and E, norovirus, herpes simplex virus, and chickenpox are highlighted, alongside seasonal respiratory infections. Temperature fluctuations drive spikes, while warmer weather boosts mosquito breeding, fueling dengue and chikungunya outbreaks. Typical symptoms include sudden high fever, severe joint/muscle pain, rashes, headaches, along with jaundice and fatigue for HAV/HEV infections. HAV and HEV are fecal-oral, from contaminated food or water; HAV is vaccine-preventable, HEV lacks a widely available vaccine and can be severe in pregnancy or immunocompromised individuals. Eosinophilia may accompany viral or allergic responses, with IGE testing confirming cases. Preventive measures emphasize early diagnosis, hydration, and removing stagnant water; chickenpox precautions require isolation and avoiding shared pools until all blisters crust (~7 days). Also warns against unsupervised high-dose vitamins due to health risks.",
