        "articleSummary": "Three siblings from Phước Năng commune, Da Nang, Vietnam, developed botulism after eating pickled fish on March 8. They were hospitalized March 9 with ptosis, limb weakness, and rapidly progressive respiratory failure requiring ventilation. Botulism Antitoxin Heptavalent (BAT), coordinated by WHO, was administered within 2 days. BAT was delivered to Da Nang International Airport on March 11 and sent to Da Nang Hospital for Women and Children (two cases: 7 and 11 years old) and Quang Nam Regional General Hospital (15 years old). BAT neutralizes circulating toxin, preventing further neural injury. By March 14, all three had been weaned from ventilation: the 7-year-old nearly 24 hours off support by noon; the 11-year-old weaned on March 13; the 15-year-old by morning March 14. All are stable and under close monitoring. BAT is scarce, costing about USD 8,000 per vial.",
