        ],
        "articleSummary": "Seven-region weekly surveillance (Oct 2021–May 2024) of SARS-CoV-2, influenza A/B (IAV/IBV) and RSV shows a statistically significant association only between IAV positivity and reduced SARS-CoV-2 risk. When IAV positivity sits between the 52nd and 88th percentiles, the relative risk of SARS-CoV-2 infection declines, with the lowest RR 0.58 (95% CrI 0.40–0.85) at a 5‑week lag when IAV positivity is near the 70th percentile. A Beijing-based two-pathogen mechanistic model estimates that IAV infection reduces SARS-CoV-2 incidence by 94.24% (95% CrI 88.50–99.24%), lasting about 38 days (95% CrI 35.50–41.29). Conversely, SARS-CoV-2 infection is associated with a slight increase in IAV infection. The findings suggest IAV circulation transiently dampens population-level SARS-CoV-2 transmission via ecological or immunological mechanisms.",
