        "articleSummary": "Measles cases rise in Yogyakarta Special Region, Indonesia, with hundreds of suspected and dozens of confirmed cases as of early 2026, mainly in Yogyakarta City and Sleman Regency. Researcher Bambang Edi Susyanto notes the surge persists from last year, driven by uneven immunization coverage, prior Covid-19 vaccination delays, and parental vaccine hesitancy. Early symptoms include high fever, cough, cold, red eyes, followed by a spreading facial-to-body rash; complications may include respiratory infections, dehydration, severe diarrhea, and potential permanent immune system impact. In Sleman, 33 confirmed measles and 4 rubella among 148 suspects during January–February 2026. In Yogyakarta City, by the eighth week of February 2026 there were 6 confirmed measles cases among 42 suspected. Prevention hinges on timely MR/MM immunization. Public health actions emphasize education through community health centers, hospitals, puskesmas, and schools, plus epidemiological investigations to identify transmission sources and close contacts and counter misinformation.",
